---
name: "Self Review"
description: "5-dimension self review of plan artifacts"
tags: [plan, openspec, review]
stage: plan
---
Read the current Mohist issue details before interpreting this task:

```bash
mo issue show ${{ issue.number }} --project-id ${{ project.id }}
```

<artifact id="self-review">

<task>
Review the generated plan artifacts and repair issues directly when safe.
Produce `self-review.md` with a machine-readable PASS or FAIL marker.
</task>

<dependencies>
Read `${{ openspecChangeDir }}/proposal.md`, `${{ openspecChangeDir }}/design.md`, `${{ openspecChangeDir }}/tasks.json`, and all specs under `${{ openspecChangeDir }}/specs/`.
</dependencies>

<review_criteria>
<alignment>
- Proposal addresses the actual issue.
- Every "What Changes" entry traces back to an issue requirement.
- No issue requirements are missing or misinterpreted.
</alignment>

<completeness>
- All requirements are covered by specs.
- All specs have tasks.
- Important edge cases are considered.
</completeness>

<consistency>
- Specs align with proposal Capabilities.
- Tasks reference correct spec files.
- Design aligns with specs.
- Naming is consistent.
</consistency>

<feasibility>
- Dependencies are available or created by earlier tasks.
- No circular dependencies exist.
- Task granularity is appropriate: each task is a complete feature slice.
  
  A task is too fine-grained when:
  - Its title names a technical action such as "define interface",
    "extract class", "register in DI", or "create file"
  - It only moves or renames code without delivering functionality
  - Lifecycle operations (install/start/stop/uninstall) are split into
    separate tasks
  - It is a standalone "test" task (e.g. "add tests for X") — tests belong
    inside the implementation task

  Repair: merge too-fine tasks into their feature-slice task instead of
  patching them individually.
</feasibility>

<dependency_completeness>
- Every non-first task has appropriate `dependsOn`.
- All `dependsOn` entries point to existing IDs with lower priority.
- No cycles exist.
</dependency_completeness>
</review_criteria>

<repair_policy>
If issues are found and the fix is safe, fix the affected files directly.
Do not delete artifacts. Do not make broad product or architectural changes during self-review.
</repair_policy>

<output_format>
# Self Review Report

## Result: PASS / FAIL

## Repaired Items

- [ID: item-N]
  Severity: info
  Scope: alignment | completeness | consistency | feasibility | dependencies
  Evidence: What was wrong and what was changed
  Verification: How the fix was verified
  Status: resolved

## Blocking Items

- [ID: item-N]
  Severity: blocking
  Scope: alignment | completeness | consistency | feasibility | dependencies
  Evidence: What is wrong
  SuggestedAction: What should be done
  Status: open | unresolved

## Follow-up Items

- [ID: item-N]
  Severity: follow-up
  Scope: alignment | completeness | consistency | feasibility | dependencies
  Evidence: Description
  SuggestedAction: Suggested improvement
  Status: follow-up
</output_format>

<verdict_marker>
The final line of `self-review.md` MUST be exactly one of:
- `<promise>PASS</promise>` if all checks pass.
- `<promise>FAIL</promise>` if any check fails.

Do NOT include more than one marker. Do NOT include markers in examples or quoted text.
</verdict_marker>

<output>
Write to: `${{ openspecChangeDir }}/self-review.md`
</output>

</artifact>
